[Event "French Defense: the wall and the counterpunch"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/rd263BaC"]

{ Today we learn the French Defence. This one is patient, not wild: you build a wall of pawns, let him have the space, then punch a hole in it with c five, right at the pawn on d four. } 1. e4 { He shoves his king pawn two squares into the middle. Answer with one careful step, pawn to e6 — that is the French, and it keeps d5 in reserve. [%cal Ye7e6] } 1... e6 { Up goes the first brick of the wall. It does shut your light bishop in behind it, and that bishop is the one worry in the whole French. [%csl Rc8] } (1... e5 { A perfectly good reply, and it leads to a whole different world: the open game. } 2. Nf3 { That is the open game starting for real. Today we are building the French wall instead. }) (1... c5 { The Sicilian: sharp, famous, and a completely different fight — not today's lesson. } 2. Nf3 { His knight comes out into that different fight. Save the Sicilian for another day. }) 2. d4 { He pushes his queen pawn up beside the first one and claims the whole centre. Hit straight back: pawn to d5. [%cal Yd7d5] } 2... d5 { Right back at him. Two pawns stand nose to nose, and the fight for the centre is properly on. } (2... c5 { A Sicilian idea here, but it lets his pawn simply charge past yours. } 3. d5 { He owns the whole middle now. In the French, d5 is the square that belongs to you. }) (2... Nf6 { The knight walks straight into his pawns instead of challenging them. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { He chases your knight away again and claims a great deal of space in the centre. }) 3. e5 { He pushes past and locks the centre. His two pawns make a chain: the one on d four holds up the one on e five, so d four is the brick to knock out. [%csl Rd4][%cal Yc7c5] } 3... c5 { The French counterpunch arrives. His chain hangs from the pawn on d4 — start chewing on it. [%cal Gc5d4] } (3... Bd7 { A quiet, useful move too — it develops a piece and keeps c5 ready for later. } 4. c3 c5 { And you strike with c5 anyway. Often the same house, reached through a different door. }) (3... Ne7 { Also fine — this knight often jumps to f5 later and does the very same job. } 4. c3 c5 { But the c5 punch comes first here, sooner or later, whichever knight you develop. }) (3... Nc6 { The knight sits on your own c pawn and blocks it — the c5 punch is gone for good. } 4. Nf3 { He gets to build his position in peace while your pieces stay cramped behind the wall. }) (3... f6 { The other lever against his chain, but played far too soon, before your king is safe. } 4. Bd3 { Your king side stands wide open and his bishop already grins straight at it. c5 comes first. }) (3... b6 { Too slow — c5 is the whole point of this pawn chain, and this spends the move on the queen side instead. } 4. c3 { He gets his ideal pawn centre for free: d4 and e5 both stand completely unchallenged. }) 4. c3 { He props the base of his chain. Pile on: knight to c6 leans on d4 as well. [%cal Yb8c6] } 4... Nc6 { Good. A second attacker lands on d4, and the knight develops itself in the same move. } (4... Qb6 { A good square for the queen, and we are heading there anyway, just by a different move order. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 { Same position either way — the knight and queen simply swapped the order they came out in. }) (4... cxd4 { You may trade here too, but keeping the tension a while longer costs you nothing. } 5. cxd4 { See what happens: now his other knight gets the c3 square for free. }) (4... Bd7 { A useful piece to develop later, but the fighters — knight and queen — come first. } 5. Nf3 { Bring out the fighters first: the knight to c6, then the queen to b6. }) 5. Nf3 { His knight jumps to f3 and guards d4 again. Now the queen: out to b6, a second fist aimed at d4. [%cal Gf3d4,Yd8b6] } 5... Qb6 { Queen and knight both glare at d4 now. He has to babysit that pawn for the rest of the opening. [%cal Gb6d4,Gc6d4] } (5... Bd7 { Also a real line — the bishop develops early and keeps its options open. } 6. Be2 Nge7 { Fine chess either way. Our line sends the queen to bully d4 first. }) (5... Nge7 { A fine move order too — f5 is the dream square for this knight either way. } 6. Na3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nf5 { The same song, one move later. Learn it first with the queen leading the way. }) (5... Nf6 { Look again before you play this — the pawn on e5 simply eats that knight for free. [%cal Re5f6] }) (5... cxd4 { Fine too — it just trades the tension away a move early, and you reach the same position either way. } 6. cxd4 { Play the queen to b6 next and you are straight back in our main line. }) 6. Be2 { The modest bishop move, tucking in behind the pawns. Trade on d4 now and prise the c column open for your pieces. [%cal Yc5d4] } 6... cxd4 { The trade goes through. His base pawn is gone, and the c column is yours to use. } (6... Nh6 { Careful with the move order here — the knight steps out before the trade, and his bishop takes it for free. } 7. Bxh6 gxh6 { He may rip your king side pawns open this way. Trade on d4 first, then bring the knight to h6. }) (6... Bd7 { Fine and calm, but it lets him settle in before you have asked d4 the question. } 7. O-O { But ask the pawn on d4 the question first, before he gets to castle in peace. }) (6... Nge7 { This knight heads for the wrong square — it does nothing to stop him trading on c5 and then hitting your queen with b4. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 8. b4 { His pawns arrive with a gain of time, chasing your queen straight off the board. }) 7. cxd4 { Knight to h6 now, on its way toward f5. And that pawn on d4 is not free: his queen is watching it. [%cal Yg8h6,Rd1d4] } 7... Nh6 { On its way. From h6 the knight reaches f5 next move, eyeing both d4 and e3. } (7... Nxd4 { Tempting, but that pawn is guarded twice — first by the knight, then by his queen down the open d column. } 8. Nxd4 { His knight recaptures yours. Grab the pawn back with your queen now and his queen takes her straight down the d column. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (7... Nge7 { Just as good, honestly — this knight reaches f5 by a different route. } 8. Na3 Nf5 { The same dream square, reached through the front door instead. }) (7... Bd7 { A useful piece, but a tempo slower than bringing the knight to its dream square. } 8. Nc3 { The knight wanted to jump to f5 right now, not wait another move. }) (7... Bb4+ { The check is fine, but it just forces the developing move he wanted to make anyway. } 8. Nc3 { He blocks with the knight and develops in the same move. Your bishop must soon move again. }) 8. Nc3 { He develops his last knight. Finish the trip: bring your own knight to f5. [%cal Yh6f5] } 8... Nf5 { Beautiful. Three of your pieces lean on d4, and the position stands fully equal, piece for piece and pawn for pawn. [%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4,Gb6d4] } (8... Bd7 { Too slow — the moment to jump the knight to f5 was right now. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Your king side pawns are torn open and the f5 dream is dead. He is clearly better here. }) (8... Be7 { The bishop can wait its turn, but the knight's trip to f5 cannot. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Same tear in your king side pawns. The knight had to jump to f5 first. }) 0-1


[Event "French Defense: the bishop that blocks the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/pvSngwg1"]
[FEN "r1b1kbnr/pp3ppp/1qn1p3/2ppP3/3P4/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 3 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1kbnr/pp3ppp/1qn1p3/2ppP3/3P4/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_3_6"]

{ Back to this locked, chain-versus-chain position from the French. Look sharp today: pretty is not always safe. } 6. Bd3 { The bishop stands tall on d3, blocking his own queen's view of d4. How do you punish that? [%csl Rd3] } 6... cxd4 { Yes. Open the position first and let the tension come off the board. } (6... Nh6 { The usual route to f5, but here he has a saving trade waiting on c5. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { The d4 target is gone and he is a touch more comfortable, but nothing here is actually wrong for you. }) (6... Bd7 { Right piece, wrong moment — the trade on c5 gets in first. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Again the target vanishes. Trading pawns first would have kept it on the board. }) (6... a6 { A useful waiting move, but it gives him just enough time to trade the target away. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Target gone again — the pawn trade always comes with tempo for him. }) (6... c4 { Tempting, since it gains space — but the tension sitting on d4 was your real treasure. } 7. Bc2 { He tucks the bishop safely away on c2 and stands better. Your play down the c column is gone. }) 7. cxd4 { Count the guards of d4: one lone knight, nothing more. But his bishop dreams of a check on b5 — cover that square first. [%cal Rd3b5] } 7... Bd7 { Perfect. No more b5 tricks for his bishop, and knight takes d4 is now a genuine threat. } (7... Nxd4 { One move too soon — the knight grabs d4 before b5 has been covered. } 8. Nxd4 Qxd4 9. Bb5+ { The check uncovers the whole d column behind it, straight at your queen. [%cal Gd3b5,Gd1d4] } 9... Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qxd4 { And your queen is gone — the check on b5 cleared the d column, and his queen simply recaptures for free. }) (7... Nh6 { You are missing a gift! His bishop is still blocking his own queen, so d4 hangs for the taking. } 8. Nc3 { He saves the pawn just in time. The chance has passed, and he stands better now. }) (7... Nge7 { The gift slips by — this knight does nothing about the hanging pawn on d4. } 8. Nc3 { Now the grab fails for good: his knight leaps to b5, and from there the fork on c7 would hit your king and your rook at once. }) 8. O-O { He castles, offering the d4 pawn in exchange for open lines. Take it — but with the right piece first. } 8... Nxd4 { The knight takes first. It can be traded off safely, with the queen following only afterward. } (8... Qxd4 { The wrong grabber — the queen takes before the knight has cleared the square. } 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 { Queen for a knight and a pawn is a poor trade. The knight had to take first. }) (8... Rc8 { You hesitated, and the moment to grab the pawn has already passed. } 9. Be2 { He calmly develops the bishop instead. The knight on f3 still guards d4 exactly as before, and the moment to grab it has already passed. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (8... Nge7 { Development can wait — the pawn on d4 was ripe for the taking right now. } 9. Nc3 { Guarded again, and now his knight has b5 leaps in the air too. }) (8... Be7 { The gift had a time limit, and developing here lets it expire. } 9. Be2 { Safe again — the pawn on d4 is guarded once more, and the moment has passed. }) 9. Nxd4 { He recaptures with the knight. Now yes, the queen may take: his own bishop on d3 blinds his queen to the danger. [%csl Rd3] } 9... Qxd4 { A clean pawn in the bag, and his own bishop on d three is still blocking his queen's view up the d column. [%csl Rd3] } (9... Bc5 { You poke the knight instead of taking it, and that hands him the moment he needed. } 10. Nb3 Be7 11. Be3 { The knight steps aside with a hit on your bishop, the free piece has gone, and he is comfortable again. }) 10. Nc3 { His last trick: the knight arrives with b5 dreams again. One tiny pawn move slams that door forever — which one? } 10... a6 { Exactly. No knight to b5, no bishop to b5, not ever again. A pawn up, so develop in peace and enjoy it. [%csl Ga6] } (10... Qxe5 { A second pawn, and it is playable — but it hands him real activity for it, so the position swings back to balanced. } 11. Re1 Qb8 12. Nxd5 { His knight grabs the pawn back and his rook eyes the open e column. Messy and equal — a6 first was the calmer road. }) (10... Qb6 { Safe, and still a clean pawn up — just a little slower than driving his pieces away at once. } 11. a3 { See, he has to spend a move on the little pawn push himself. Playing a6 first was the tidier way. }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop leaps forward, but it walks straight into a storm of his own pieces. } 11. Be3 { He hits back with a bishop move that gains time, and you are clearly worse here. }) 0-1


