{ Third visit to this locked centre. Sometimes he tries something sly with a pawn move instead. } 6. a3 { The point of a3 is b4 next: chase your queen away and grab space on the queen side. How do you take that idea away for good? [%cal Rb2b4] } 6... c4 { Frozen. His b4 push is off the table for good, and b3 is now a hole your knight can visit. [%csl Gb3] } (6... a6 { Playable and calm, though it does nothing yet about his b4 idea. } 7. Bd3 { But his b4 plan stays fully alive after this. Stamping on c4 was the move that killed it. }) (6... a5 { A polite way to stop b4, at the cost of a small hole on b5 later. } 7. Bd3 { Decent, though that a5 pawn may itch later on. The c4 stamp is the cleaner way to stop him. }) (6... Bd7 { Too slow — his b4 push is still on the table if you do not stamp on c4 right now. } 7. b4 { There is the push, exactly what c4 was meant to deny him forever. }) (6... f6 { A real lever against his chain, and playable here too. } 7. Bd3 { A different fight entirely. Our plan freezes the queen side first instead. }) (6... cxd4 { The trade helps him more than it helps you here. } 7. cxd4 { After a3, his b pawn is free to roll forward and gain space, since nothing of yours holds it back any longer. }) (6... Nh6 { The old route to f5, but one beat too late once his knight has settled on d2. } 7. b4 { The space grab arrives for free. Playing c4 was the move to deny it. }) 7. Nbd2 { His knight ducks to the quiet d2 square. There is still a hole waiting on b3 for one of your pieces — which one heads there? [%csl Gb3] } 7... Na5 { The knight heads for b3. His whole queen side already aches from the squeeze. } (7... f6 { Also strong — the centre lever works here too, just on a different wing. } 8. Be2 { Both plans exist for you. This chapter marches on the queen side instead. }) (7... Bd7 { First the knight! Playing the bishop here lets him break the clamp with b3. } 8. b3 { He breaks the clamp at once. Knight to a5 first would have kept c4 rock solid. }) (7... Nh6 { The king side knight can wait its turn — the queen side clamp with a5 needs finishing first. } 8. b3 { Again the clamp cracks open. a5 first was the move! }) 8. Be2 { He tucks the bishop quietly back to e2. One of your own pieces still needs a job before the pawns can run — which one? } 8... Bd7 { Good. From d7 the bishop can swing out to a4 later and trade itself for his knight. } (8... Ne7 { Also natural — your pieces do not care which order they come out in. } 9. h4 { The pieces come in either order here. We bring the bishop out first. }) (8... h6 { A useful breath of a move, though the queen side race is where the real fight is. } 9. Rb1 { Never wrong to ask a question like this, but the queen side race is still the main event. }) 9. Rb1 { He tucks the rook behind his b pawn, still hoping for b3 one day. One more piece wants to join the party before you strike — where does the last knight belong? } 9... Ne7 { Set. His queen side plan is dead for good, no b4 ever, while your knight already eyes the hole on b3. } (9... Qc7 { Wise. She steps off the b column early, out of the coming storm. } 10. Nf1 Nb3 { And look: the knight lands right in the hole on b3. Both roads get you there. }) (9... O-O-O { Brave and strong — the king marches straight into the storm side. } 10. Qc2 { A rich fight follows, and you are the one pressing. }) (9... h6 { A fine waiting move before you commit to anything. } 10. h4 { The real business here is still b5 and a5, not the king side. }) (9... a6 { Useful someday, but not urgent right now with the queen side calling. } 10. h4 { But the queen side push is where the real show is happening. }) 0-1


[Event "French Defense: the exchange mirror"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/tYB3quJi"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3p4/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
