{ Sometimes he ducks the fight and swaps pawns in the middle instead. A mirror position, but only one of you walks in with a plan. } 3. exd5 { He trades right away in the centre. Which piece keeps your pawn shape the cleanest afterward? } 3... exd5 { Good. The pawn recaptures, your queen stays safely at home, and your shape stays clean. } (3... Qxd5 { The queen strolls out into the open far too early here. } 4. Nf3 { He develops a piece for free while she wanders around looking for a safe home. Recapturing with the pawn was the move. }) 4. Nf3 { A mirror now — he develops the very knight you are about to. What is the natural way to answer, piece for piece? } 4... Nf6 { Copied calmly. Development comes first here, before anything sharp is tried. } (4... Nc6 { Fine as well — this knight develops just as naturally. } 5. Nc3 { Just mind the bishop pin on b5 that these mirror positions often bring. }) (4... Bd6 { Good square for the bishop. We simply bring both knights out before either bishop. } 5. c4 { And see how the early poke at c4 starts asking real questions of your pawn on d5. }) (4... Be7 { A shade shy of its best square, but nothing wrong with it. } 5. Bd3 { d6 is the fighting square for that bishop — from there it stares at his king side too. }) (4... Bg4 { A real pin on his knight, and a fair try. But there is a sharp answer waiting for it. } 5. h3 Be6 { He backs the bishop off rather than press the pin further, and you stand a shade more comfortable than in our main line. }) 5. Bd3 { His bishop comes out, eyeing your king side. Where does yours belong in this same mirror? } 5... Bd6 { Good square. Your bishop stares right back at his king side in return. } (5... c5 { The French break works here too, and it is completely sound chess. } 6. c3 { It opens the game at once. Our line keeps the mirror going one more beat first. }) (5... Bg4 { The right idea, just one move too early — your king is still standing in the centre. } 6. O-O { Castle first, then pin the knight in comfort with nothing to fear. }) (5... Be7 { Too modest — from e7 this bishop only watches the game, while d6 lets it fight in it. } 6. h3 { On d6 the bishop fights for the game. On e7 it only watches it. }) 6. O-O { He tucks his king away into safety. What is the safest reply in a mirror position like this? } 6... O-O { King safe before any fight starts. Mirror positions stay balanced when you match him move for move. } (6... Bg4 { The pin one beat too soon — your king is still standing in the middle of the board. } 7. Re1+ Be7 { The check forces your other bishop onto the awkward e7 square to block, instead of its favourite post on d6. Castling first avoids all of this. }) (6... Be6 { Solid enough, though it blocks your own c pawn from ever reaching c5. } 7. Nc3 { But the king was still waiting his turn to castle. }) (6... Nc6 { This knight takes the c6 square away from your own c pawn. } 7. Re1+ Be7 { And the same check forces your other bishop off its favourite post on d6 and onto the passive e7 square instead. }) 7. Bg5 { The first real question of the game: he pins your knight to the queen behind it. Do you have the same trick ready? } 7... Bg4 { Mirrored. His knight is pinned to his own queen too now, and he must be just as careful with it. } (7... Be6 { A calm choice too, and every bit as good as the pin. } 8. Re1 { A long, quiet game follows. The pin on g4 bites harder than this. }) (7... h6 { You may poke the bishop with a pawn first if you like. } 8. Bh4 { The bishop steps back and keeps on pinning. No harm done either way. }) (7... c6 { Sturdy, but it lets him keep a pin on you for free while you pin nothing back. } 8. Nbd2 { He improves his own position for nothing in return — the mirror favoured mirroring. }) (7... Nbd7 { Playable, though his coming c4 poke gets a little annoying for this knight soon. } 8. c4 { Pin his knight first with your bishop, then bring this knight out afterward. }) 8. Nbd2 { He breaks the pin politely with his own knight. One more piece wants to copy the mirror — which one, and where does it go? } 8... Nbd7 { Done. Your bishop on d six points at h two, the other one pins his knight on f three, and the position is dead level — exactly what this mirror was built to reach. } (8... Bxf3 { Taking the knight is playable, but it hands over your good bishop and lets him out of the pin for free. } 9. Nxf3 Nbd7 10. c3 { Level, though he keeps two bishops against your one for the rest of the game. }) (8... c5 { Breaking the mirror at once, and objectively the most challenging try for Black. } 9. h3 Bh5 { Keep the pin and head for a rich, open game. Ours is the quieter road to the same kind of position. }) (8... h6 { Fine — it just asks the bishop a question before anything sharp happens. } 9. Bh4 { Everything holds together — the pin stays on and nothing is lost by the small pause. }) (8... Re8 { A good rook move too, preparing the centre for later. } 9. h3 { Your other knight was itching to develop first, but no harm comes from this order either. }) 0-1


