{ Sometimes he tucks the knight to d2 instead, hiding it away from any pin. } 3. Nd2 { The knight ducks to d2, safe from any pin. But hiding has a price: it stands passively there for now. What move of yours hits e4 right away? } 3... Nf6 { Straight at e4. Between your knight and the d5 pawn, that square now has two attackers and only one defender. } (3... c5 { A great line in its own right, and a very natural try. } 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. exd5 exd5 { An open world with free pieces flying everywhere, but a lot of theory to learn. }) (3... dxe4 { Also sound. It melts straight into the trade line from the knight c3 chapter you already know. } 4. Nxe4 { Same furniture as before: knight to d7, a trade, then the c5 punch. }) (3... Nc6 { This knight sits on your own c pawn, and c5 is the whole lungs of the French. } 4. c3 { He builds his position in peace while you stay cramped. Keep the c6 square free for the pawn instead. }) (3... a6 { A clever waiting system, and a real one — but its own course of study for another day. } 4. Ngf3 { Today we keep it classic and head for our usual squares. }) (3... Be7 { Solid and slow, and it gives up the sharper punch at e4. } 4. Ngf3 { The knight punch at e4 is the more testing reply here. }) 4. e5 { The pawn charges at your knight, gaining space and asking a question. Where is the safe square that still bites back at e5? } 4... Nfd7 { Snug. From d7 the knight leans on e5, and the c5 punch comes next move. [%cal Gd7e5] } (4... Ne4 { Brave, but nobody is holding this knight's hand out there. } 5. Nxe4 dxe4 { He just takes the knight, and the stray pawn left behind on e4 becomes a real worry. The retreat kept everything clean. }) (4... Ng8 { All the way home again — two whole moves spent for nothing. } 5. Ndf3 { He gets to develop happily while you catch up. }) (4... c5 { Your own knight! It is standing right in the pawn's mouth. } 5. exf6 Qxf6 { A whole knight for a single pawn — an expensive way to learn the lesson. }) 5. Bd3 { The bishop takes the pretty diagonal, eyeing your king side. Which pawn break have you used in every chapter so far? [%csl Rd4] } 5... c5 { The French counterpunch again. His centre pawn on d4 is the target once more. } (5... b6 { The poor French bishop dreams of reaching a6 this way. A real idea, just at the wrong moment. } 6. c3 { Strike with c5 before anything else — that bishop plan can wait. }) (5... f6 { Too soon. Your king side pawns open up while your king is still at home to feel it. } 6. Qh5+ { The queen crashes straight in through the hole you just opened. c5 first, f6 much later. }) 6. c3 { He props up d4 to keep his chain solid. Which piece leans on that pawn next? } 6... Nc6 { Good. A second attacker lands on d4, just like in every chapter before this one. } (6... cxd4 { Fine — it just trades a move early and reaches the very same position either way. } 7. cxd4 Nc6 { Same house, different door. The knight and the f6 punch still come regardless. }) (6... Qb6 { The old reflex from the other chapters! Playable, but here d4 is already well fed. } 7. Ne2 { Our real key move in this one is the f6 lever instead. }) 7. Ne2 { A clever square for the knight — it leaves f3 open for the other one. What is left hanging in the centre now? } 7... cxd4 { The trade goes through, clearing the tension before the next lever arrives. } (7... Qb6 { Fine, though it commits the queen before you even know if you will need her there. } 8. Nf3 { But see how well fed d4 already is. The f6 lever is the real door in this one. }) (7... f6 { The right idea, but the wrong order — the tension on d4 should come off the board first. } 8. Nf4 { Now this knight has found a dream square, and your e6 pawn aches for it. Trading pawns first would have stopped this. }) 8. cxd4 { Locked again, chain against chain. This position has a second lever besides c5 — do you remember it? [%csl Re5] } 8... f6 { The second lever: f6, striking straight at the base of his chain on e5. } (8... Qb6 { The queen glares at d4, but it holds firm anyway. } 9. Nf3 { The f6 break is the real door here, not the queen. }) (8... Be7 { Too quiet — hit the chain now, while his king still sits in the middle. } 9. O-O { He castles comfortably away, and your moment to strike thins out. }) 9. exf6 { He must trade, or the pawn falls for nothing. Which piece recaptures so it lands on an active square? } 9... Nxf6 { Good. The knight lands back in the game, active and ready to fight. } (9... Qxf6 { The queen grabs it instead. Natural enough, but she stands tall and exposed in the open wind. } 10. O-O { The knight belonged on f6, shielding your king — castle first next time. }) 10. Nf3 { See why e2 was the clever square? The knight slots in cleanly now. Where does your last bishop want to fight from? } 10... Bd6 { The fighting post. From d6 the bishop glares straight at his king side. } (10... Qc7 { A natural home for the queen too, eyeing h2 somewhere down the road. } 11. Nc3 { Also fine for you, just a touch less precise than developing the bishop first. }) (10... Bb4+ { The check trades your good bishop away for nothing much in return. } 11. Bd2 { He happily offers the swap. Keep that bishop instead — d6 is its throne. }) (10... Be7 { Solid, but on d6 the very same bishop glares straight at his king side. } 11. O-O { A tempo shy of the best set-up, but nothing is actually wrong. }) 11. O-O { Kings must hide away in positions like this one. Time for yours too? } 11... O-O { Your rook looks down the f column at his knight on f three, your bishop on d six points at his king side, and your king is tucked away safe. } (11... Qc7 { Fighting spirit, but castle first — that open column cuts both ways in the middlegame. } 12. Nc3 { He gains a move on you while your own king still waits out in the draft. }) (11... Bd7 { Castle before the lines open up around your king. } 12. Bf4 { He reaches the good bishop square first, since your king still has not castled. }) 0-1


