{ Last door of this repertoire. Whatever he tries here, our answer is the simplest chess in the book. } 3. Nc3 { His knight now defends that pawn on e4, his most popular try. Which pawn grabs it anyway and trades all the fuss away? } 3... dxe4 { Good. The pawn comes off cleanly, and the whole position simplifies right away. } (3... Nf6 { The Classical — excellent and rich, and a whole world of its own theory. } 4. Bg5 { The famous pin, and hours of homework that come with it. Our trade keeps tonight's lesson short. }) (3... Bb4 { The Winawer — sharp, famous, and double-edged from the very first moves. } 4. e5 { Both kings end up running for their lives in that one. Learn it someday; today we keep things clean. }) (3... Be7 { Too polite a square for the bishop — both the Winawer and the Classical fight harder than this. } 4. e5 { He grabs space for free while your bishop sits doing very little. }) (3... Nc6 { This knight sits on your own c pawn — no c5 later means no real French fight at all. } 4. e5 { He clamps the centre shut, and your counterplay never really arrives. }) 4. Nxe4 { He recaptures in the centre. One knight move now sets up the rest of this whole plan — can you see which one? } 4... Nd7 { The key move. This knight exists for exactly one job: taking back on f6 later. } (4... Nf6 { Natural enough, but watch what happens to your pawns after the trade. } 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 { Cracked. That is why the knight goes to d seven first: it takes back on f six and your pawns stay whole. }) (4... Bd7 { The fortress plan: tuck the bishop behind the wall on c6. Solid and slow, but perfectly sound. } 5. Bd3 Bc6 { Playable, and a real plan. Ours simply frees your pieces faster. }) (4... Be7 { Quiet, but it skips the whole reason the knight went to d7 in the first place. } 5. Nf3 { The d7 knight plan is the smoother of the two roads here. }) 5. Nf3 { He develops his other knight. Bring your last knight out too — where does it offer a trade on your own terms? } 5... Ngf6 { Right knight. This one offers the trade, and the d7 knight stays ready to recapture. } (5... Ndf6 { The wrong horse! This one exists purely to take back later, not to trade itself off now. } 6. Ng3 { He dodges the trade, and now your two knights trip over each other's squares. }) (5... Be7 { Fine, though it skips the trade this whole line was built around. } 6. Bd3 { The knight hop to f6 is more to the point than this bishop move. }) 6. Nxf6+ { He trades on your terms after all. Which knight was born to recapture here? } 6... Nxf6 { That is the whole point of putting a knight on d7 — a clean, active recapture. } (6... Qxf6 { The queen takes instead, and promptly becomes the target herself. } 7. Bg5 { With tempo! She has to run again while he develops another piece for free. }) (6... gxf6 { Your king side pawns crack open for absolutely nothing in return. } 7. g3 { He will aim at those cracked holes forever now. The knight was placed on d7 to avoid exactly this. }) 7. Bd3 { Pieces keep coming off, but the plan stays the same. Which pawn break have you played in almost every chapter now? } 7... c5 { The move you know by heart. His centre pawn on d4 is the target, same as always. } (7... Bd7 { Equally good — the bishop heads for c6 instead, aiming straight through the centre at his king side. } 8. O-O { We strike with the pawn first, while his centre still stands tall and inviting. }) (7... b6 { Freeing the French bishop — a lovely idea, just one beat too late to matter here. } 8. c3 { He cements d4 with a second pawn, and your c5 break gets harder to land. }) (7... Be7 { Castling first is sound, sensible chess — the pawn break can wait one more move. } 8. Qe2 { Fine, but c5 was the real moment to strike, not this. }) 8. dxc5 { He trades rather than defend the pawn. Which piece recaptures and comes out fighting at the same time? } 8... Bxc5 { Good. The bishop recaptures and comes out fighting right away. } (8... Qc7 { You will win the pawn back eventually, but why wander with the queen when the bishop does the job at once? } 9. b4 { He clings onto the pawn and gains space doing it. Recapturing at once was simpler. }) (8... Be7 { You do get the c5 square back eventually, just by a slower road. } 9. O-O { But the bishop stood prouder and more active sitting on c5 itself. }) 9. Bf4 { His bishop eyes the c7 square. Is that really anything to fear right now? } 9... O-O { King safe, pieces free to develop, and no locked pawn chain left to trap that last bishop — this French never even got the chance to go wrong. } (9... Qb6 { Just as strong a square: both his b2 and f2 pawns twitch from here. } 10. O-O { He castles and dares you to take on b two: his rook swings to b one, his bishop drops to e five, and your queen ends up the one running. }) (9... Nd5 { Poking the bishop with the knight, and it simply steps back unbothered. } 10. Bg3 { Nothing gained by the poke, and your knight has left its good post for no reason. }) (9... Bd7 { Fine, though his knight lands hard on e5 next move as a result. } 10. Ne5 { Castle first, and kick the bishop around later once your king is safe. }) 0-1
